.TH LORD 1 "" "New Human Shell"
.SH NAME
law, lawd, lord, lorde, lordhavemercy, lordylordydaddysforty \- run a prose program with [STRANGE-GODS-REDACTED]
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B law
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.br
.B lawd
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.br
.B lord
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.br
.B lorde
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.br
.B lordhavemercy
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.br
.B lordylordydaddysforty
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ request " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The runners supply the prose program as
.B developer_instructions
to
.B [STRANGE-GODS-REDACTED] exec
in the current directory. Any project
.I AGENTS.md
stays in place. An optional leading
.B \-\-
separates the runner invocation from the request. The runners join the
request arguments with spaces and use
.B @
as the request if it is empty. Quote a multiline request as one argument
to retain its line breaks. Standard input can supply data for a request.
For a standalone invocation with terminal input and redirected or piped
output, the runner reads from
.I /dev/null
so the filter can run without waiting for terminal input.
.PP
The runners require
.B [STRANGE-GODS-REDACTED]
on
.BR PATH .
They select the [STRANGE-GODS-REDACTED] sandbox mode
.B danger-full-access
and use the invoking user's filesystem permissions. They allow execution
outside a Git repository. They capture both backend output streams until
[STRANGE-GODS-REDACTED] exits. On success, they relay the backend's standard output.
On failure, they relay its standard error and return its status.
.SH REASONING EFFORT
Each runner sets
.B model_reasoning_effort
to the value shown below.
.TP
.B law
.B low
with the model selected by
.BR NSH_FAST_MODEL .
.TP
.B lawd
.B low
with the configured model.
.TP
.B lord
.B medium
with the configured model.
.TP
.B lorde
.B high
with the configured model.
.TP
.B lordhavemercy
.B xhigh
(extra-high) with the configured model. This is the default for
.BR LD (1).
.TP
.B lordylordydaddysforty
.B max
(maximum) with the configured model.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NSH_PROGRAM
Path to a readable regular file containing the prose program. If unset
or empty, the runner looks for an executable
.B @
on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B NSH_FAST_MODEL
Model used by
.BR law .
If unset or empty, defaults to
.BR gpt-6-luna .
.TP
.B NSH_TURN
The shell host sets this to 1, invokes the runner with
.B "@@ \-\-"
followed by the input line, and supplies turn context on standard input.
With both the value 1 and this argument prefix, the runner passes
.B \-\-ephemeral
to [STRANGE-GODS-REDACTED] and prefixes the request with the runner name and a user-input
label. The turn context remains on standard input.
.SH HOSTED USE
For each delegated turn, including an answer to a pending question, the
shell host starts at
.B law
and tries the next runner in the order above only when the reply consists of
.BR @more .
Standalone invocations run the selected runner once.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The runner returns 2 for an unknown invocation name or an invalid or
unreadable prose program file. It returns 127 if it needs to find
.B @
on
.B PATH
and cannot find it. A failing [STRANGE-GODS-REDACTED] invocation supplies its own exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
lord 'summarize the input' < notes.txt
NSH_PROGRAM=./instructions lorde 'check the examples'
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR @ (1),
.BR LD (1)
